(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["breakthroughs",{"l:breakthroughs/2017-attention-is-all-you-need":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Before 2017, language models read text one word at a time with recurrent networks, which was slow to train and forgot early words. The \u003cb>transformer\u003c/b> dropped recurrence and used only \u003cb>attention\u003c/b>: every token looks directly at every other token, and all positions are processed in parallel. That made training on GPUs at enormous scale practical, and every modern LLM descends from it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The problem: reading one word at a time\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard model for text in 2016 was the \u003cb>recurrent neural network (RNN)\u003c/b>, usually an LSTM. It keeps a hidden state, a vector that summarises everything read so far, and updates it once per word. Two things follow from that design:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>No parallelism across positions.\u003c/b> To process word 100 you must first process words 1 to 99, so training cannot use the thousands of parallel cores on a GPU well.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A memory bottleneck.\u003c/b> Information from word 1 has to survive 99 updates of one fixed-size vector to influence word 100. Long-range links fade.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Translation systems already used an add-on called \u003cb>attention\u003c/b> (2014): when writing each output word, the decoder looked back over all the input words and weighted the useful ones. The 2017 paper's title is its argument: keep the attention, throw away the recurrence.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The idea: attention only\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each token is turned into three vectors: a \u003cb>query\u003c/b> (what am I looking for?), a \u003cb>key\u003c/b> (what do I offer?) and a \u003cb>value\u003c/b> (what I pass on if chosen). A token's query is compared with every key using a dot product; a softmax turns the scores into weights that sum to 1; the token's new representation is the weighted sum of the values. The Inside an LLM module works through this with real numbers.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An illustrative example: in \"The animal didn't cross the street because it was too tired\", the token \"it\" might put most of its weight on \"animal\":\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Token attended to\u003c/th>\u003cth>The\u003c/th>\u003cth>animal\u003c/th>\u003cth>street\u003c/th>\u003cth>tired\u003c/th>\u003cth>all others\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Weight from \"it\" (illustrative)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.02\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.61\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.09\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.23\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.05\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The paper's Table 1 states the trade-off precisely, for sequence length \u003ci>n\u003c/i> and vector size \u003ci>d\u003c/i>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Layer type\u003c/th>\u003cth>Work per layer\u003c/th>\u003cth>Sequential steps\u003c/th>\u003cth>Longest path between two tokens\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Recurrent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n × d²\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Self-attention\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n² × d\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Attention needs no sequential steps and connects any two tokens in one hop. The price is the \u003cb>n²\u003c/b> term: 1,000 tokens means a million token pairs per layer, and 100,000 tokens means ten billion. Much of the rest of this module is the story of taming that cost.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The results were immediate. The transformer set a new state of the art in English-to-German translation (28.4 BLEU) and reached 41.8 BLEU on English-to-French after training for 3.5 days on eight GPUs, a small fraction of the cost of the previous best systems.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>From encoder-decoder to decoder-only\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original transformer had two halves: an \u003cb>encoder\u003c/b> that reads the whole source sentence (every token sees every other token) and a \u003cb>decoder\u003c/b> that writes the translation one token at a time, with a \u003cb>causal mask\u003c/b> so each position only sees earlier positions. Within a year the field split the halves apart:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>2017 Transformer\u003c/b>\u003csmall>encoder + decoder\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>2018 BERT\u003c/b>\u003csmall>encoder only\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Embedding, reranking and classification models\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>2017 Transformer\u003c/b>\u003csmall>encoder + decoder\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>2018 GPT\u003c/b>\u003csmall>decoder only\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Every chat LLM you call today\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Decoder-only won for language models because it is one stack with one objective, predict the next token, and every task (translation, question answering, code) can be written as text to continue. The architecture of a 2026 frontier model is still recognisably the 2017 decoder, with better position encoding, normalisation, attention variants and mixture-of-experts layers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long prompts cost more than linearly in attention work.\u003c/b> This is why very long inputs slow down the first token (the Inference module explains prefill).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Order must be added.\u003c/b> Attention by itself does not know word order, so models add position information; the method used today, RoPE, is also how context windows get extended.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Encoders are alive.\u003c/b> The embedding models and rerankers in the Retrieval stage are often BERT-style encoders, because reading both directions at once is ideal for understanding a passage.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which two weaknesses of recurrent networks did the transformer remove?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Recurrent networks process tokens one after another, so training cannot be parallelised across positions; and information from early tokens must pass through many updates of one fixed-size state, so long-range links fade. Self-attention processes all positions at once and connects any two tokens in a single step.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is self-attention's cost quadratic in sequence length, and why does that matter in practice?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every token's query is compared with every token's key, so n tokens give n × n scores per head per layer. Doubling the prompt roughly quadruples that work, which is why long contexts are slow and expensive and why so much later work (FlashAttention, sparse and linear attention) targets it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did decoder-only models, rather than encoder-decoder or encoder-only, become the basis of LLMs?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A decoder trained to predict the next token can generate text, uses one simple objective on any raw text, and can express every task as a continuation of a prompt. Encoder-only models cannot generate, and encoder-decoder models add a second stack without a clear benefit for general-purpose text generation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2018-to-2020-pretraining-at-scale":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pretraining\u003c/b> means training one large model on huge amounts of unlabelled text with a simple self-supervised objective, then adapting it to many tasks. BERT filled in hidden words and became the best model for understanding text; GPT predicted the next word and could generate. At GPT-3's scale, a new ability appeared: learning a task from a few examples inside the prompt, with no training at all.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Before: one model per task\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Until 2018, each language task (sentiment, question answering, named entities) usually needed its own labelled dataset and its own model. Labels are slow and expensive, so most of the world's text was useless for training. The insight of 2018 was that raw text already contains a training signal: hide part of it and ask the model to predict it. No labels needed, so you can use billions of words.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Two ways to pretrain\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>BERT (Google, Oct 2018)\u003c/th>\u003cth>GPT (OpenAI, June 2018 onwards)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Objective\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Masked language modelling: hide about 15% of tokens, predict them using both sides\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Next-token prediction: predict each token from the ones before it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Architecture\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Transformer encoder\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Transformer decoder with a causal mask\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strength\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Understanding: classification, search, extraction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Generation: writing, continuing, answering\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>How you used it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Add a small output layer and fine-tune per task\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fine-tune (GPT-1), later just prompt (GPT-3)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Descendants in 2026\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Embedding models, rerankers, fast classifiers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Every chat and reasoning LLM\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>BERT dominated 2019: it set new records on eleven benchmarks at once. But GPT's objective turned out to scale better, because generating text is a more general skill than filling blanks.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The GPT line\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>GPT-1 (2018):\u003c/b> a 12-layer decoder, about 117M parameters, pretrained on BooksCorpus (over 7,000 unpublished books), then fine-tuned for each task.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>GPT-2 (Feb 2019):\u003c/b> 1.5B parameters, trained on WebText, about 40 GB of text from 8 million web pages linked from Reddit posts with at least 3 karma. Without any fine-tuning it could do rough summarisation, translation and question answering just from how the prompt was phrased. OpenAI released it in stages, citing misuse concerns.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>GPT-3 (May 2020):\u003c/b> 175B parameters, about 300B training tokens, and the paper that made \u003cb>in-context learning\u003c/b> famous.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In-context learning\u003c/h4>\u003cp>GPT-3 was tested by putting a task description and a few examples in the prompt and letting it continue:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Translate English to French:\nsea otter =&gt; loutre de mer\npeppermint =&gt; menthe poivrée\ncheese =&gt;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The model continues with \" fromage\". Nothing in the model changes: no gradient updates, no fine-tuning. The \"learning\" happens inside one forward pass, because the model has seen countless documents where a pattern is set up and then continued. The paper defined three settings: \u003cb>zero-shot\u003c/b> (instruction only), \u003cb>one-shot\u003c/b> (one example) and \u003cb>few-shot\u003c/b> (typically 10 to 100 examples, as many as fit in the 2,048-token window). Performance rose with more examples and, much more steeply, with model size.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why it mattered\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In-context learning turned a model into a general-purpose engine: one set of weights, thousands of tasks, each defined by text. That made it possible to sell a model as an API, and it made \u003cb>prompting\u003c/b> a form of programming. It also had a clear limit: GPT-3 continued text rather than following instructions, so prompts had to be cleverly shaped documents. Fixing that is the story of the instruction-following lesson.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Few-shot examples are still one of your strongest levers.\u003c/b> Two or three good examples often fix format and style faster than paragraphs of instructions (Prompting module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Base models continue; they do not obey.\u003c/b> If you ever use a base model (for fine-tuning or completion), you must write prompts as documents to continue.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pick the right descendant.\u003c/b> For search, deduplication or a cheap classifier, a small BERT-style encoder can be far faster and cheaper than an LLM.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between BERT's and GPT's pretraining objectives?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>BERT hides about 15% of the tokens and predicts them using context on both sides (masked language modelling) with an encoder. GPT predicts each next token from only the tokens before it (causal language modelling) with a decoder, which also lets it generate text.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is in-context learning, and what changes inside the model when it happens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is a model performing a new task from instructions or examples placed in the prompt, such as translating after seeing three English-French pairs. Nothing in the weights changes; the behaviour comes from the forward pass conditioning on the pattern in the context.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name one job in 2026 where a BERT-style model is still the better choice than a GPT-style LLM.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Producing embeddings for search, reranking retrieved passages, or high-volume classification such as spam or intent detection, where a small encoder is accurate enough and much faster and cheaper.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:scaling-laws":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In 2020, OpenAI showed that a language model's loss falls as a smooth \u003cb>power law\u003c/b> as you add parameters, data or compute, so you can predict a huge model's quality from small experiments. In 2022, DeepMind's Chinchilla paper corrected the recipe: for a fixed compute budget, grow parameters and training tokens together, about \u003cb>20 tokens per parameter\u003c/b>. Today labs deliberately train far past that point, because a smaller model is cheaper to serve for its whole life.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Power laws: predictable progress\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Kaplan and colleagues trained many models of different sizes and found that test loss follows simple curves over more than seven orders of magnitude. For model size \u003ci>N\u003c/i> (parameters, excluding embeddings), with enough data:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>L(N) = (Nc / N) ^ 0.076\n\n10x more parameters -&gt; loss multiplied by 10 ^ -0.076 ≈ 0.84 (about 16% lower)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Similar laws held for dataset size and for compute, and details such as depth versus width mattered little. The practical consequence was enormous: a lab could train a ladder of small models, fit the line, and predict what a model 100 times bigger would achieve before spending the money. Scaling laws are why companies were willing to invest in ever larger training runs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The compute budget: C ≈ 6ND\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Training compute in floating-point operations (FLOPs) is roughly six times parameters times tokens: about 2 FLOPs per parameter per token for the forward pass and 4 for the backward pass.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>C ≈ 6 × N × D\nLlama 3 405B: 6 × 405e9 × 15.6e12 ≈ 3.8e25 FLOPs (the paper reports 3.8 × 10^25)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Kaplan's recipe versus Chinchilla's\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a budget C, you can buy a bigger model trained on fewer tokens, or a smaller model trained on more. Kaplan's fits suggested spending most extra compute on parameters (optimal size growing as about C^0.73). Following that advice and GPT-3's setup, most large models of 2020 to 2021 were trained on about 300B tokens: GPT-3 saw roughly 1.7 tokens per parameter.\u003c/p>\u003cp>DeepMind trained over 400 models, from 70M to 16B parameters, on 5B to 500B tokens, and found that parameters and tokens should grow in \u003cb>equal proportion\u003c/b>: double the model, double the data. Its Table 3:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Parameters\u003c/th>\u003cth>Compute-optimal tokens\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens per parameter\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20.2B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>205.1B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>21\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>67B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.5T\u003c/td>\u003ctd>22\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>175B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3.7T\u003c/td>\u003ctd>21\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>To test it, they trained \u003cb>Chinchilla\u003c/b> (70B parameters, 1.4T tokens) with the same compute as their own \u003cb>Gopher\u003c/b> (280B parameters, 300B tokens). Chinchilla beat Gopher, GPT-3 and Megatron-Turing NLG 530B across the board, reaching 67.5% on MMLU, more than 7 points above Gopher, while being four times cheaper to run.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A worked example. With a budget of 1e23 FLOPs and D = 20N:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>C = 6 × N × 20N = 120 N²\nN = sqrt(1e23 / 120) ≈ 2.9e10   -&gt; about 29B parameters\nD = 20 × N         ≈ 5.8e11   -&gt; about 580B tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why today's models are \"over-trained\"\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compute-optimal means the lowest loss for a given \u003ci>training\u003c/i> budget. But a model is trained once and then serves billions of requests, and serving cost scales with parameters (about 2N FLOPs per generated token). So labs now pick a smaller model and train it far longer than Chinchilla suggests, accepting a less efficient training run in exchange for cheap inference:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\u003cth>Parameters\u003c/th>\u003cth>Training tokens\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens per parameter\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>GPT-3 (2020)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>175B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~300B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~1.7\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Chinchilla (2022)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>70B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.4T\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Llama 3 405B (2024)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>405B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>15.6T\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~39\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Qwen3-0.6B (2025)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.6B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~36T\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~60,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The loss keeps falling past the compute-optimal point, just more slowly. That is good news for you: the small models you can run on a laptop are far better than their size suggests.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bigger is not automatically better.\u003c/b> A well-trained smaller model can beat a larger under-trained one, so compare models on your eval, not their parameter count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scaling laws now apply beyond pretraining.\u003c/b> Reasoning models showed similar smooth gains from reinforcement learning compute and from thinking longer at answer time (later lessons).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>6ND is your back-of-envelope tool\u003c/b> for any training job, including the fine-tuning budgets in the Training module.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain the compute-optimal trade-off between parameters and tokens.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Training compute is roughly C ≈ 6 × parameters × tokens, so for a fixed budget you trade model size against data. Chinchilla showed that loss is lowest when both grow in equal proportion, about 20 tokens per parameter: a 70B model on 1.4T tokens beat the 280B Gopher trained on 300B tokens with the same compute. A model that is too big for its data is under-trained and wastes compute; one that is too small saturates. In practice labs now over-train smaller models on far more tokens because inference cost depends on model size.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Roughly how many FLOPs does it take to train a 7B model on 2T tokens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>6 × 7e9 × 2e12 = 8.4e22 FLOPs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">GPT-3 was trained on about 300B tokens. By the Chinchilla rule, was it under- or over-trained, and by how much?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Under-trained: 300B / 175B is about 1.7 tokens per parameter, roughly twelve times fewer than the ~20 the rule recommends (about 3.5T tokens for 175B parameters).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why would a lab train a 0.6B model on tens of trillions of tokens if that is far from compute-optimal?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Because lifetime cost is dominated by serving, which scales with parameters. Extra training keeps lowering the loss, so a small, heavily trained model gives the best quality per unit of inference cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2022-following-instructions-instructgpt-rlhf-and":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>GPT-3 continued text; it did not reliably do what you asked. In 2022 OpenAI's \u003cb>InstructGPT\u003c/b> fine-tuned it on answers written by people, then on people's rankings of answers using \u003cb>reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)\u003c/b>. Labellers preferred a 1.3B InstructGPT over the 175B GPT-3. \u003cb>ChatGPT\u003c/b> (30 November 2022) applied the same recipe to dialogue and turned LLMs into a mass-market product.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The problem: helpful text is rare on the internet\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A pretrained model imitates the internet. Ask GPT-3 \"Explain the moon landing to a 6 year old\" (the example in the InstructGPT paper's Figure 2) and a plausible continuation is a list of similar questions, because that is what such a line is often followed by on the web:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Prompt: Explain the moon landing to a 6 year old.\nBase model (illustrative): Explain the theory of gravity to a 6 year old.\nExplain the theory of relativity to a 6 year old in a few sentences.\nExplain the big bang theory to a 6 year old.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Making more of the model would not fix this. The model knew enough to answer; it did not know that answering was the job. The paper's first sentence says it plainly: making language models bigger does not inherently make them better at following a user's intent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The three-step recipe\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>1. Supervised fine-tuning\u003c/b>\u003csmall>labellers write ideal answers (about 13k prompts)\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>2. Reward model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>labellers rank 4 to 9 answers per prompt (about 33k prompts)\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>3. Reinforcement learning\u003c/b>\u003csmall>PPO pushes the model toward high reward (about 31k prompts)\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Supervised fine-tuning (SFT).\u003c/b> A team of about 40 contractors wrote high-quality answers to real prompts sent to the OpenAI API and to prompts they wrote themselves. Fine-tuning GPT-3 on these demonstrations teaches the format: read the instruction, answer it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reward model.\u003c/b> For each prompt the model generates several answers and labellers rank them. Ranking is faster and more consistent than writing. A 6B model is trained to output a score such that preferred answers score higher. It becomes an automatic, tireless stand-in for the labellers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reinforcement learning.\u003c/b> The SFT model generates answers, the reward model scores them, and an algorithm called \u003cb>PPO\u003c/b> (proximal policy optimisation) nudges the model towards higher-scoring answers. A penalty keeps it close to the SFT model so it cannot drift into gibberish that happens to fool the reward model.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>The Training module explains each step's maths (the reward model's loss, the KL penalty) and the simpler alternatives that followed, such as DPO.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The results\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In human evaluations on the API prompt distribution, outputs from the \u003cb>1.3B InstructGPT were preferred to the 175B GPT-3\u003c/b>, despite having 100 times fewer parameters. InstructGPT was also more truthful and less toxic. There was a cost the authors called the \u003cb>alignment tax\u003c/b>: scores on some academic benchmarks dropped slightly. Mixing a little pretraining data into the RL step (a variant called PPO-ptx) recovered most of it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The lesson that shaped the industry: pretraining provides knowledge and skills; post-training decides how the model \u003ci>behaves\u003c/i>. A small amount of carefully collected human data, tens of thousands of examples against hundreds of billions of pretraining tokens, changed the model's usefulness more than a 100x size increase.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The ChatGPT moment\u003c/h4>\u003cp>ChatGPT, released on 30 November 2022, was a model from the GPT-3.5 series trained with the same recipe on conversations, wrapped in a free chat interface. It had no new architecture. What was new was that anyone could talk to an instruction-following model, and it reached an estimated 100 million monthly users within about two months. Every major lab shipped a chat assistant within the following year, and \"chat model\" became the default thing APIs serve.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Roles and chat templates come from here.\u003c/b> The system, user and assistant messages you send are rendered into special tokens the model saw during post-training (Inside an LLM module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Behaviour is a training artefact.\u003c/b> Tone, verbosity, refusals, formatting habits and the tendency to agree with you (sycophancy) all come from post-training data and reward models, which is why models of similar size feel so different.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Your fine-tuning follows the same pattern.\u003c/b> SFT on demonstrations, then preference tuning, is still the standard recipe (Fine-tuning module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What are the three steps of the InstructGPT recipe?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(1) Supervised fine-tuning on answers written by human labellers; (2) training a reward model on labellers' rankings of several model answers per prompt; (3) reinforcement learning with PPO to maximise the reward model's score, with a penalty for drifting far from the supervised model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How could a 1.3B model be preferred over a 175B model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Users judge whether the model does what they asked. The 175B base model had more knowledge but often continued text instead of answering; the small model had been trained to follow instructions in the expected format, which mattered more to the raters than raw capability.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the \"alignment tax\", and how did InstructGPT reduce it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is the drop in performance on some standard benchmarks caused by alignment training. InstructGPT reduced it by mixing pretraining-style updates into the reinforcement learning step (PPO-ptx).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2022-chain-of-thought-react-and-toolformer":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Three papers from 2022 and early 2023 showed that models do better when they write out intermediate steps (\u003cb>chain-of-thought\u003c/b>), when they interleave those thoughts with actions such as searches and read the results (\u003cb>ReAct\u003c/b>), and when they learn to call tools such as a calculator (\u003cb>Toolformer\u003c/b>). Together they are the blueprint for today's reasoning models and agents.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Chain-of-thought: show your working\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The chain-of-thought paper's Figure 1 uses a simple word problem. With ordinary few-shot examples (question, then answer) the model answers directly and gets it wrong. With few-shot examples that show the reasoning, the model imitates the reasoning and gets it right:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Q: The cafeteria had 23 apples. If they used 20 to make lunch and bought 6 more,\n   how many apples do they have?\n\nStandard prompting:    A: The answer is 27.\nChain-of-thought:      A: The cafeteria had 23 apples originally. They used 20 to make\n                       lunch, so they had 23 - 20 = 3. They bought 6 more, so they have\n                       3 + 6 = 9. The answer is 9.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With just eight such examples, the 540B PaLM model reached state of the art on GSM8K (grade-school maths word problems). A few months later another paper found you did not even need examples: adding \"Let's think step by step\" raised one InstructGPT model from 17.7% to 78.7% on the MultiArith benchmark and from 10.4% to 40.7% on GSM8K. A third, \u003cb>self-consistency\u003c/b>, sampled many chains and took a majority vote over the final answers, adding another 17.9 points on GSM8K.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why it works: a transformer spends a fixed amount of computation per generated token. Writing out steps gives the model more tokens, so more computation, and turns intermediate results into context it can read back, like a scratchpad. The benefit appeared mainly in large models; small models wrote fluent but wrong chains.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>ReAct: think, act, observe\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reasoning alone can still invent facts. ReAct let the model alternate between a thought, an action against an external source and an observation of the result. A trace in the style of the paper's HotpotQA examples:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Question: Which magazine was started first, Arthur's Magazine or First for Women?\nThought 1: I need to search both magazines and compare their start dates.\nAction 1: Search[Arthur's Magazine]\nObservation 1: Arthur's Magazine was an American literary periodical published 1844-1846...\nThought 2: Now First for Women.\nAction 2: Search[First for Women]\nObservation 2: First for Women is a woman's magazine ... started in 1989.\nThought 3: 1844 is earlier than 1989, so Arthur's Magazine was first.\nAction 3: Finish[Arthur's Magazine]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The application parses each \u003ccode>Action\u003c/code>, runs it, and appends the \u003ccode>Observation\u003c/code> to the prompt before asking for the next step. Grounding thoughts in a simple Wikipedia API reduced hallucination on question answering, and on the interactive benchmarks ALFWorld and WebShop ReAct beat imitation- and RL-trained baselines by 34 and 10 absolute points of success rate with only one or two examples in the prompt.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Toolformer: learning when to call a tool\u003c/h4>\u003cp>ReAct relied on prompting. Meta's \u003cb>Toolformer\u003c/b> (February 2023) taught a 6.7B GPT-J model to insert API calls such as \u003ccode>[Calculator(400 / 1400) → 0.29]\u003c/code> into its own text. The training data was self-generated: sample candidate calls, execute them, and keep only those whose results made the following tokens easier to predict. Its tools were a calculator, a question-answering system, two search engines, a translator and a calendar. The resulting model often beat the much larger GPT-3 on tasks that needed those tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What happened next\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>In 2023, model APIs added native \u003cb>function calling\u003c/b>: you describe tools in a JSON schema and the model returns a structured tool call instead of text you must parse. Every agent framework is a ReAct loop on top of it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In 2024 and 2025, \u003cb>reasoning models\u003c/b> were trained to produce long chains of thought by themselves (the reasoning models lesson), so the prompting trick became a built-in, trained behaviour.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For fast, non-reasoning models, asking for step-by-step reasoning before the answer still helps on multi-step problems. For reasoning models it is redundant; give goals and constraints instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Asking for the final answer in a fixed place (\"The answer is ...\") makes outputs easy to check and to majority-vote.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A written chain of thought is not guaranteed to be the real cause of the answer. Verify answers; do not treat the reasoning as an audit log.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does chain-of-thought prompting improve accuracy on multi-step problems?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each generated token gets a fixed amount of computation, so writing intermediate steps gives the model more computation and lets it read its own intermediate results back as context, like a scratchpad. Answering immediately forces the whole calculation into one token's worth of work.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In a ReAct loop, who produces the Observation, and why does that reduce hallucination?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The application does: it parses the model's Action, executes it (for example a search) and inserts the real result into the context. The model's next thought is then based on retrieved facts rather than on what it remembers or invents.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How did Toolformer decide which tool calls to keep in its training data?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It sampled candidate API calls in text, executed them, and kept only the calls whose results reduced the model's loss on predicting the following tokens, so the model learnt to call tools only when they helped.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2022-to-2023-inference-gets-cheap-flashattention":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two systems ideas made running LLMs much cheaper without changing a single output token. \u003cb>FlashAttention\u003c/b> (2022) computes exact attention in tiles that fit in the GPU's small, fast on-chip memory, so the huge matrix of attention scores never travels to slow memory. \u003cb>PagedAttention\u003c/b> (2023, the core of vLLM) stores each request's KV cache in small fixed-size blocks, like an operating system's memory pages, so one GPU can serve many more requests at once.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Problem 1: attention was limited by memory traffic, not maths\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A GPU has two kinds of memory. The FlashAttention paper's Figure 1 gives figures for an A100: about 20 MB of on-chip \u003cb>SRAM\u003c/b> at around 19 TB/s, and 40 GB of \u003cb>HBM\u003c/b> (the main GPU memory) at about 1.5 TB/s. Standard attention computed the full score matrix QKᵀ, wrote it to HBM, read it back to apply softmax, wrote the result, and read it again to multiply by V.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The matrix is big. For 8,192 tokens, one attention head in one layer has 8,192 × 8,192 ≈ 67 million scores, about 134 MB in 16-bit floats, and a model has dozens of heads and layers. Most of the time went on moving this matrix, not on arithmetic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>FlashAttention: tile it and never store the matrix\u003c/h4>\u003cp>FlashAttention splits Q, K and V into blocks that fit in SRAM. For each block of queries it streams through the blocks of keys and values, computing partial scores and keeping a running maximum and running sum so the softmax can be corrected as new blocks arrive (the \"online softmax\" trick). The full n × n matrix never exists in HBM.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Standard:  Q,K -&gt; S = QKᵀ (n×n, write to HBM) -&gt; softmax (read, write) -&gt; P·V (read) -&gt; output\nFlash:     for each Q block: for each K,V block: compute in SRAM, update running max/sum -&gt; output\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Key properties:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exact.\u003c/b> Same result as standard attention, up to floating-point rounding. It is not an approximation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Memory linear in sequence length\u003c/b> instead of quadratic, which made long contexts trainable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Faster.\u003c/b> The paper reports 3x faster GPT-2 training at 1K sequence length and a 15% end-to-end speedup on BERT-large. FlashAttention-2 (2023) and FlashAttention-3 (2024, for H100 GPUs with FP8 support) pushed further, and these kernels are now standard inside PyTorch, vLLM, SGLang and most training stacks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The arithmetic is still quadratic; FlashAttention removed the memory wall, not the n² compute.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Problem 2: serving wasted most of its KV-cache memory\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When a model generates text, it keeps the keys and values of every previous token in every layer, the \u003cb>KV cache\u003c/b> (the Inference module covers it in depth), so it does not recompute them. Before vLLM, serving systems reserved one contiguous chunk per request big enough for the maximum possible length. Most of it sat empty. The vLLM authors measured that only \u003cb>20.4% to 38.2%\u003c/b> of KV-cache memory in existing systems held actual token data. Less memory per request means fewer requests in a batch, and batch size is what makes serving cheap.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>PagedAttention: virtual memory for the KV cache\u003c/h4>\u003cp>PagedAttention splits each request's cache into blocks of a few tokens (for example 16) and keeps a \u003cb>block table\u003c/b> mapping logical blocks to physical blocks anywhere in GPU memory, exactly as an operating system maps virtual pages:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Request A: logical [0][1][2]  -&gt; block table: 0-&gt;7, 1-&gt;2, 2-&gt;9\nRequest B: logical [0][1]     -&gt; block table: 0-&gt;7 (shared prefix), 1-&gt;4\nGPU memory blocks:  [ ][ ][A1][ ][B1][ ][ ][A0=B0][ ][A2]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Blocks are allocated only as tokens are generated, so waste is under one block per request. Requests that start with the same prompt can point at the same physical blocks and copy only when they diverge. vLLM combined this with \u003cb>continuous batching\u003c/b> (adding and removing requests from the running batch at every step) and reported 2 to 4 times the throughput of FasterTransformer and Orca at the same latency.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cheaper tokens.\u003c/b> These kernels and schedulers, plus continuous batching and quantisation, are a big part of why the price of a given level of capability kept falling.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prompt caching rests on this.\u003c/b> Block-level KV reuse across requests is the mechanism under provider prompt-caching discounts (Billing module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use a real serving engine.\u003c/b> If you self-host, vLLM or SGLang give you all of this; a naive generate loop does not (Serving module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Does FlashAttention approximate attention to save time?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No. It computes exactly the same attention, but in tiles held in fast on-chip SRAM with an online softmax, so the full score matrix is never written to slow HBM. The saving is memory traffic and memory size, not arithmetic.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What waste did PagedAttention remove, and why does that increase throughput?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pre-allocating a contiguous, maximum-length KV-cache region per request left most of it unused (only 20 to 38% held tokens). Allocating small blocks on demand means far more requests fit in GPU memory at once, and larger batches give more tokens per second from the same GPU.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does block-based storage make it easy to share a common prompt prefix between requests?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each request's block table can point at the same physical blocks for the shared prefix, so the KV cache for that prefix is stored once; a request copies a block only when its tokens start to differ.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2023-open-weights-and-cheap-fine-tuning-llama-mi":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In 2023 strong model weights became downloadable (LLaMA, Llama 2, Mistral 7B), and \u003cb>LoRA\u003c/b> and \u003cb>QLoRA\u003c/b> made it possible to fine-tune them on a single GPU by training small low-rank adapters on top of frozen, even 4-bit, weights. Together they created the open-model ecosystem: thousands of fine-tunes, local inference on laptops, and today's open-weight models that trail the frontier by months.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The open-weights wave\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>LLaMA (Meta, February 2023):\u003c/b> models from 7B to 65B parameters, trained only on publicly available data for trillions of tokens. The headline result was the Chinchilla lesson in action: \u003cb>LLaMA-13B outperformed GPT-3 (175B) on most benchmarks\u003c/b>, because it was trained on far more tokens per parameter. Weights were released to researchers and soon leaked publicly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Alpaca (Stanford, March 2023):\u003c/b> fine-tuned LLaMA 7B on 52K instruction-following examples generated with OpenAI's text-davinci-003 for under $500 of API cost, and behaved qualitatively like it in early tests. Instruction tuning had become something a university lab could afford.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Llama 2 (July 2023)\u003c/b> came with a licence allowing commercial use, and \u003cb>Mistral 7B (September 2023)\u003c/b>, under Apache 2.0, outperformed Llama 2 13B on every benchmark its authors evaluated, using grouped-query attention and sliding-window attention for cheaper inference.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Open weights were only half the story. Fully fine-tuning even a 7B model was out of reach for most people.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why full fine-tuning is expensive\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Training with the Adam optimiser in mixed precision needs roughly 16 bytes per parameter: 16-bit weights (2) and gradients (2), a 32-bit master copy of the weights (4), and Adam's two 32-bit running averages (8), before counting activations.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>7B model, full fine-tune:  7e9 × 16 bytes ≈ 112 GB   -&gt; several 80 GB GPUs\n65B model, full fine-tune: 65e9 × 16 bytes ≈ 1 TB    -&gt; a cluster\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>LoRA: train a small correction, not the whole matrix\u003c/h4>\u003cp>LoRA (2021) freezes the pretrained weight matrix W and learns a low-rank update beside it: W' = W + B·A, where B is d × r and A is r × d, with a small rank r such as 8 or 16. Only A and B are trained.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>One 4096 × 4096 attention projection:\n  full update: 4096 × 4096       = 16,777,216 trainable values\n  LoRA, r = 8: 2 × 4096 × 8      =     65,536 trainable values (0.39%)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Gradients and optimiser state are needed only for the adapters, so memory falls sharply. On GPT-3 175B the paper reports \u003cb>10,000 times fewer trainable parameters and 3 times less GPU memory\u003c/b> than full fine-tuning, with quality on par. The adapter is a file of a few megabytes to a few hundred megabytes; one base model can serve many adapters, one per customer or task.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>QLoRA: do it on a 4-bit base model\u003c/h4>\u003cp>QLoRA (May 2023) stores the frozen base model in 4 bits and back-propagates through it into LoRA adapters. Three tricks keep quality at the level of 16-bit fine-tuning: \u003cb>NF4\u003c/b>, a 4-bit data type suited to normally distributed weights; \u003cb>double quantisation\u003c/b>, which also compresses the scaling constants; and \u003cb>paged optimisers\u003c/b>, which spill optimiser state to CPU memory during spikes.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>65B base model at 4 bits: 65e9 × 0.5 bytes ≈ 33 GB  -&gt; fits on one 48 GB GPU with adapters\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The authors fine-tuned over 1,000 models this way. Their best, Guanaco, reached 99.3% of ChatGPT's score on the Vicuna benchmark after 24 hours on a single GPU.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>LoRA is the default way to fine-tune open models.\u003c/b> Hugging Face PEFT and TRL, Unsloth and Axolotl all build on it; the Fine-tuning module walks through a run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fine-tuning is for behaviour, not facts.\u003c/b> Adapters are great at format, tone and narrow tasks; for knowledge, retrieval usually works better.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Serving engines can host many adapters on one base model\u003c/b>, so per-customer customisation is cheap.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Licences differ.\u003c/b> Llama-family licences carry conditions; many Mistral, Qwen and DeepSeek releases are Apache 2.0 or MIT (Landscape module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How many trainable values does LoRA add to a 4096 × 4096 weight matrix at rank 16, and what fraction is that?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>2 × 4096 × 16 = 131,072, about 0.78% of the matrix's 16.8 million values.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can QLoRA fine-tune a 65B model on one 48 GB GPU when full fine-tuning would need about a terabyte?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The frozen base weights are stored in 4 bits (about 33 GB), gradients and optimiser state exist only for the small adapters, and paged optimisers absorb memory spikes, so the whole job fits in 48 GB.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did LLaMA-13B outperform the much larger GPT-3?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It was trained on far more tokens per parameter (about a trillion tokens for 13B parameters versus about 300B tokens for 175B), following the Chinchilla insight that under-trained large models waste their size.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2023-to-2025-mixture-of-experts-and-long-context":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>mixture-of-experts (MoE)\u003c/b> model has many feed-forward \"experts\" per layer and a router that sends each token to only a few, so the model holds many parameters but each token pays for only the active ones. Long context came from three fixes: stretching position encodings (RoPE scaling), memory-efficient attention, and shrinking the KV cache (GQA, MLA). By 2025 most frontier open models were MoE with context windows of 128K tokens or more.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Mixture of experts: big model, small bill\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In a normal transformer every token passes through the same feed-forward block in each layer. An MoE layer replaces that block with several experts and a small \u003cb>router\u003c/b> network:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Token vector\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Router\u003c/b>\u003csmall>scores each expert\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Top-2 experts run\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Weighted sum of their outputs\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Next layer\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Mixtral 8x7B\u003c/b> (December 2023) made this mainstream for open models: 8 experts per layer, 2 chosen per token, \u003cb>47B total parameters but 13B active\u003c/b>. (Not 56B, because attention and embeddings are shared.) It matched or beat Llama 2 70B and GPT-3.5 on the benchmarks its authors evaluated, while computing like a 13B model per token.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The idea was old: a 1991 paper proposed mixtures of experts, and a 2017 Google paper built \"outrageously large\" sparse MoE layers. What changed was engineering: load balancing so experts are used evenly, stable training and efficient serving. DeepSeek pushed the design further with \u003cb>fine-grained experts\u003c/b> (many small experts, more of them chosen per token) plus \u003cb>shared experts\u003c/b> that every token uses. DeepSeek-V3 (December 2024) had \u003cb>671B total and 37B active\u003c/b> parameters, was pretrained on 14.8T tokens, and its report gives 2.788M H800 GPU hours for the full training run, about $5.6M at an assumed $2 per GPU hour (excluding earlier research and experiments).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Dense 70B\u003c/th>\u003cth>MoE, 671B total / 37B active\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Memory to hold weights (16-bit)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~140 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~1.3 TB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Compute per token\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~70B parameters' worth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~37B parameters' worth\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Knowledge capacity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lower\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Much higher\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Total parameters decide how much memory you need; active parameters decide speed and cost per token.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Long context: three obstacles\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Positions beyond training length.\u003c/b> Models use \u003cb>RoPE\u003c/b> (rotary position embeddings), which rotate query and key vectors by an angle that depends on position. Positions far beyond those seen in training produce rotations the model has never seen, and attention breaks down. \u003cb>Position Interpolation\u003c/b> (2023) squeezed new positions into the trained range, extending LLaMA to 32,768 tokens with under 1,000 fine-tuning steps; \u003cb>YaRN\u003c/b> (2023) did it with 10 times fewer tokens and 2.5 times fewer steps than earlier methods. Long-context training phases now use variants of these.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Attention cost.\u003c/b> FlashAttention made attention memory linear in length (previous lesson); the compute is still quadratic.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>KV-cache size.\u003c/b> The cache grows with every token. \u003cb>Grouped-query attention (GQA)\u003c/b> shares keys and values across groups of heads; DeepSeek's \u003cb>multi-head latent attention (MLA)\u003c/b> compresses them into a small latent vector, cutting DeepSeek-V2's KV cache by 93.3% compared with its earlier 67B model.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Year\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003cth>Context window\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2020\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GPT-3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,048 tokens\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2023\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LLaMA (February)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,048 tokens\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Llama 3 405B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128K tokens\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Gemini 1.5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1M tokens in production; near-perfect retrieval up to 10M in research tests\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Late 2026\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many frontier and open models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1M tokens is common\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Read model cards for total versus active parameters.\u003c/b> An MoE model can be fast per token yet need many GPUs to self-host.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A long window is not the same as good use of it.\u003c/b> Recall degrades as inputs grow (\"context rot\", Context module), so retrieval and careful context assembly still matter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long prompts cost money and latency\u003c/b> on every call unless you use prompt caching (Billing module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Mixtral has 47B total and 13B active parameters. Which number sets GPU memory and which sets speed?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The 47B total must be held in memory, because any token may be routed to any expert. The 13B active parameters are what each token is computed through, so they set compute per token, speed and serving cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can you not simply feed a model inputs longer than it was trained on?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its position encoding (RoPE) produces rotation angles for those positions that it never saw in training, so attention scores become unreliable and quality collapses. Extending context needs interpolation methods such as Position Interpolation or YaRN plus some training on long documents, and enough memory for the larger KV cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does multi-head latent attention reduce, and why does that matter for serving?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It compresses the keys and values stored per token into a small latent vector, shrinking the KV cache (by 93.3% in DeepSeek-V2). A smaller cache lets a GPU hold longer contexts and more concurrent requests, which lowers cost per token.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2024-to-2025-reasoning-models":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In September 2024 OpenAI's \u003cb>o1\u003c/b> showed that a model trained with reinforcement learning to produce a long hidden chain of thought gets much better at maths, code and science, and keeps improving the longer it thinks. In January 2025 \u003cb>DeepSeek-R1\u003c/b> published an open recipe: reinforcement learning with automatically checkable rewards on a strong base model. Within months every major lab offered a reasoning mode, and \"how hard should the model think\" became a dial in every API.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Chain-of-thought prompting helped, but models had never been \u003ci>trained\u003c/i> to reason well. They rarely backtracked, rarely checked their work, and errors compounded. Meanwhile, making pretraining bigger was getting very expensive. The question was whether you could buy accuracy another way.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>o1: thinking as a trained skill\u003c/h4>\u003cp>OpenAI released o1-preview on 12 September 2024. It generates many hidden reasoning tokens before its visible answer. OpenAI's announcement showed two smooth curves: accuracy rose with more reinforcement learning compute during training, and also with more thinking at answer time. That second curve made \u003cb>test-time compute\u003c/b> a new scaling axis. Reasoning tokens are billed as output tokens, which is why a short answer from a reasoning model can be expensive.\u003c/p>\u003cp>OpenAI did not publish the recipe.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>DeepSeek-R1: the open recipe\u003c/h4>\u003cp>DeepSeek released R1 on 20 January 2025 under the MIT licence, with a detailed paper.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>R1-Zero\u003c/b> applied reinforcement learning directly to the DeepSeek-V3 base model, with no supervised examples of reasoning. The rewards were rules, not a learnt reward model:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Accuracy reward:\u003c/b> maths answers in a required format (for example a box) compared with the known answer; code compiled and run against test cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Format reward:\u003c/b> the reasoning must sit between \u003ccode>&lt;think&gt;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>&lt;/think&gt;\u003c/code> tags.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Problem with a checkable answer\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model samples a group of solutions\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Rule-based checker\u003c/b>\u003csmall>right or wrong\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Rewards\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Update toward better-than-average solutions\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Over thousands of RL steps, R1-Zero's pass@1 on the AIME 2024 maths competition rose from 15.6% to 71.0% (86.7% with majority voting). Its answers grew longer on their own, and the paper describes an \"aha moment\" where an intermediate checkpoint learnt to stop, re-evaluate its approach and allocate more thinking time. Nobody wrote examples of that behaviour; the reward produced it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>R1-Zero's reasoning was hard to read and mixed languages, so \u003cb>R1\u003c/b> added stages: a few thousand curated \"cold-start\" examples, reasoning RL, about 800K new training samples (roughly 600K reasoning traces filtered by rejection sampling plus 200K general ones), supervised fine-tuning, then a final RL stage for helpfulness and harmlessness. It performed comparably to OpenAI's o1 on reasoning benchmarks.\u003c/p>\u003cp>DeepSeek also fine-tuned six small dense models (1.5B to 70B, based on Qwen and Llama) on R1's outputs. The distilled 7B model scored 55.5% on AIME 2024. The Training module covers each stage, including the GRPO algorithm.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why it mattered\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>It showed \u003cb>reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards\u003c/b> works at scale: if you can check an answer automatically, you can train reasoning without human-written reasoning.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It made reasoning cheap and open. R1 briefly became the top free app on the US App Store, and Nvidia's share price fell about 18% in a day as investors reconsidered how much compute frontier AI needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Through 2025 every major lab shipped reasoning or \"thinking\" modes, often in hybrid models that can switch thinking on and off.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Choose effort deliberately.\u003c/b> Reasoning helps maths, code, planning and agent decisions; it adds latency and cost on simple tasks (Landscape module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Small distilled reasoning models\u003c/b> can run locally and handle surprisingly hard problems.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Agents got much better\u003c/b> because deciding the next step in a long task is exactly what reasoning training improves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Thinking text is not an explanation.\u003c/b> Summaries of reasoning are not faithful logs; verify outputs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is a verifiable reward, and why did it make large-scale reinforcement learning for reasoning practical?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A reward computed by a program that checks the answer, for example comparing a maths result with the known solution or running code against tests. It is cheap, needs no human labels and is much harder to fool than a learnt reward model, so it can be applied to millions of attempts.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between DeepSeek-R1-Zero and DeepSeek-R1?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>R1-Zero was trained with reinforcement learning straight from the base model, with no supervised data; it reasoned well but its output was hard to read and mixed languages. R1 added a small cold-start supervised set, then reasoning RL, rejection-sampled supervised fine-tuning on about 800K samples, and a final RL stage, giving readable, generally helpful outputs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why might you choose a distilled small reasoning model over a frontier reasoning model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It can run locally or cheaply, keeps data private, and handles many maths, code and logic tasks well enough, at a fraction of the latency and cost, as long as your eval shows it clears the bar.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2024-to-2025-agents-get-standards":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Between 2023 and 2025, tool use moved from text that applications had to parse (the ReAct style) to a trained, native capability: structured function calls, operating a computer from screenshots, and coding agents that work for many minutes in a real repository. Standards grew around it: \u003cb>MCP\u003c/b> for connecting tools and data, \u003cb>AGENTS.md\u003c/b> and \u003cb>skills\u003c/b> for instructions, now governed by a neutral foundation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>From parsing text to native tool calls\u003c/h4>\u003cp>ReAct-style agents asked the model to print \u003ccode>Action: Search[...]\u003c/code> and hoped the application could parse it. In 2023 model APIs added \u003cb>function calling\u003c/b>: you send JSON Schema descriptions of your tools, and the model replies with a structured call (\u003ccode>{\"name\": \"get_order\", \"arguments\": {\"id\": \"A123\"}}\u003c/code>) that your code executes, sending the result back as a tool message. Labs then trained models specifically on tool-use data, so calls became reliable enough to chain dozens of them. The Tools module teaches the loop in detail.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>decides on a tool call\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Your code\u003c/b>\u003csmall>runs the tool\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>reads the result\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Answer or next tool call\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The milestones\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>When\u003c/th>\u003cth>Milestone\u003c/th>\u003cth>What changed\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2023\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Function calling in major chat APIs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tool calls as structured JSON, not parsed text\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Oct 2023\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SWE-bench\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,294 real GitHub issues from 12 Python repositories became the yardstick for coding agents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Oct 2024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Computer use (Claude 3.5 Sonnet, public beta)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model looks at screenshots and moves the mouse and keyboard like a person\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Nov 2024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Model Context Protocol (MCP) from Anthropic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One open protocol for connecting any AI application to any tool or data source\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Feb to May 2025\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude Code, OpenAI Codex CLI, GitHub Copilot coding agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agents that read a repository, edit files, run tests and open pull requests\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mar 2025\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI adopts MCP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>MCP becomes the cross-vendor default\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Late 2025\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agent Skills; MCP donated to the Agentic AI Foundation (Linux Foundation)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reusable instruction packages; neutral governance\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why tools needed a standard\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Without a standard, every AI application needs a custom integration for every system: N applications times M tools means N × M connectors. MCP turns this into N + M. Each application implements an MCP client once; each system exposes an MCP server once.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Host app\u003c/b>\u003csmall>Claude, an IDE, your agent\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>MCP client\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>MCP server\u003c/b>\u003csmall>tools, resources, prompts\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Your database, API or files\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>An MCP server describes its tools (with schemas), resources (readable data) and prompts. Any MCP-capable host can then use it. By late 2026 MCP is hosted by the Agentic AI Foundation under the Linux Foundation, alongside AGENTS.md (a Markdown file of instructions for coding agents in a repository) and the A2A agent-to-agent protocol. The MCP module covers the current specification, which as of late 2026 is the 2026-07-28 version.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why coding agents came first\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Coding was the first domain where agents became genuinely useful, for the same reason reasoning RL worked on code: \u003cb>a verifiable signal\u003c/b>. Tests pass or fail, the compiler complains, the linter reports. An agent can try, check and fix in a loop without a human judging every step. A coding agent is a loop around the model plus a \u003cb>harness\u003c/b>: tools to read and edit files and run commands, permission rules, a sandbox, and context management for long sessions. Teams found the harness mattered almost as much as the model, which is why the Agents stage has whole modules on loops and harnesses.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it means for you today\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expose your systems as tools with clear schemas\u003c/b>, and consider an MCP server so many clients can use them (Atlas does this in the MCP module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Design for loops.\u003c/b> Agent quality comes from feedback signals (tests, validators, checks) the agent can use on its own.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tools plus untrusted input means risk.\u003c/b> An agent that reads a web page or email and can also send data is open to prompt injection (Security module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Write instructions where agents look.\u003c/b> An AGENTS.md file and well-scoped skills make coding agents far more effective in your repository.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What integration problem does MCP solve, in one sentence with numbers?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Instead of N AI applications each building custom connectors to M systems (N × M integrations), each application implements an MCP client once and each system exposes an MCP server once, giving N + M.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did coding become the first domain where autonomous agents worked well?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Code gives cheap, automatic feedback: tests, compilers and linters tell the agent whether a change works, so it can iterate without a human checking each step. The same verifiable signal also made code a good target for reinforcement learning.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How does function calling differ from computer use?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With function calling the model chooses from tools you define with schemas and returns structured arguments for your code to run. With computer use the model operates a graphical interface itself from screenshots, issuing mouse and keyboard actions, which works with any application but is slower and less reliable.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:breakthroughs/2025-to-2026-where-the-frontier-is-now":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>As of late 2026, progress comes less from one big idea and more from many compounding ones. Models are \u003cb>very sparse MoE\u003c/b> with \u003cb>hybrid attention\u003c/b> for cheap long context, trained and served in \u003cb>8-bit and 4-bit\u003c/b> precision, and trained with RL to work on \u003cb>long agentic tasks\u003c/b>. On the engineering side the craft has shifted from prompts to \u003cb>context engineering\u003c/b>, skills and harnesses. The best open-weight models trail the closed frontier by months.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Models: very sparse and very long\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Specifics change monthly; these are examples as of late 2026, from the model cards.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model (open weights)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Total / active parameters\u003c/th>\u003cth>Active share\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notable features\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DeepSeek-V4-Pro\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.6T / 49B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~3%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1M-token context, FP4 and FP8 mixed precision, compressed sparse attention\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Qwen3.8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.4T / 95B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~4%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>512 experts (10 routed plus 1 shared per token), hybrid linear and full attention, 262K native context, about 1M extended\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>gpt-oss-120b (Aug 2025)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>117B / 5.1B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~4%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>MXFP4 4-bit expert weights so it fits on one 80 GB GPU, Apache 2.0\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Compare Mixtral's 2 of 8 experts (25% of expert weights per token) in 2023 with roughly 3 to 4% active now: models store far more knowledge per unit of compute. Other families at the frontier of open weights include Kimi (Moonshot), GLM (Zhipu) and Mistral; on the closed side, Anthropic, OpenAI, Google and xAI ship new versions every few months, with 1M-token windows now common.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Hybrid attention: fixed-size memory for most layers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Full attention keeps keys and values for every past token, so memory and per-token work grow with context. \u003cb>Linear attention\u003c/b> layers (for example Gated DeltaNet, used in Qwen3.8) instead keep a fixed-size state that is updated with each token, like an RNN, so their cost per token does not grow with length. The weakness is precision: a fixed-size state cannot recall every exact detail from far back. Hybrids interleave them, for example three linear-attention layers for every full-attention layer, keeping a few layers that can look anything up exactly. DeepSeek took a different route with compressed and sparse attention; its V4 report gives inference FLOPs at 27% and KV cache at 10% of the previous version at 1M tokens.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Low precision everywhere\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Memory per parameter is the main cost of holding a model:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>bytes per parameter:  BF16 = 2   FP8 = 1   FP4 = 0.5\n1.6T parameters:      3.2 TB     1.6 TB    0.8 TB\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>DeepSeek-V3 showed FP8 training at scale in 2024; by 2026 FP8 training and FP4 serving are mainstream, supported by new GPU generations. Each halving of precision roughly halves memory and memory traffic, which in turn lowers cost per token. Quality loss is small when done carefully, but you should still check it on your eval.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reasoning for long tasks\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reinforcement learning has moved from maths puzzles to \u003cb>agentic environments\u003c/b>: real repositories, browsers, terminals and tool APIs with automatic checks. Models now sustain tasks over hours, and providers ship server-side \u003cb>compaction\u003c/b> that summarises older context so a session can continue past the window. Other efficiency ideas from 2025 to 2026 that you will see on model cards: \u003cb>multi-token prediction\u003c/b> (training the model to predict several future tokens, which also enables faster speculative decoding) and serving systems that split prefill and decode across machines.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Engineering: context, skills and harnesses\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Context engineering\u003c/b> has replaced prompt engineering as the core skill: deciding which tokens (instructions, tools, retrieved data, memory, history) go into each call, because models get worse as context fills up (\"context rot\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Agent Skills\u003c/b> are folders with a \u003ccode>SKILL.md\u003c/code> file plus optional scripts and references that an agent loads only when relevant (progressive disclosure); originally from Anthropic, now an open cross-vendor format.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Harness engineering\u003c/b> is the work on the loop around the model: tools, permissions, sandboxing, compaction and memory. Major vendors now offer hosted harnesses as well as SDKs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>MCP\u003c/b> keeps evolving; the 2026-07-28 specification made the protocol stateless. The Agents stage covers all of these.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>How to read a new announcement\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>You see\u003c/th>\u003cth>It means\u003c/th>\u003cth>Check\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"X total, Y active\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>MoE: memory follows X, speed and price follow Y\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can you host X?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Hybrid linear attention\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cheaper long context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Recall on your long inputs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"FP4\" or \"MXFP4\" weights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fits on fewer GPUs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quality on your eval\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Thinking budget\" or \"effort\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reasoning dial\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cost and latency per task\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"1M context\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fits huge inputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Effective accuracy at your lengths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do hybrid models mix linear-attention layers with some full-attention layers?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Linear attention keeps a fixed-size state, so its cost and memory per token stay constant as context grows, which makes long context cheap. But a fixed state cannot recall every exact detail, so a minority of full-attention layers is kept to look up precise information anywhere in the context.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Roughly how much memory do the weights of a 1.6T-parameter model need at FP4, and at BF16?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>1.6e12 × 0.5 bytes ≈ 0.8 TB at FP4, and 1.6e12 × 2 bytes ≈ 3.2 TB at BF16, before any KV cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Model names change monthly. What is the lasting skill this module is trying to give you?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Recognising the underlying ideas (sparsity, attention variants, precision, post-training, test-time compute, tool standards), knowing which bottleneck each removes, and checking any claim against your own eval and constraints rather than the announcement.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:breakthroughs/base-instruct-and-reasoning-models-side-by-side":"\u003cp>You will run one small model family locally in three modes: the \u003cb>base\u003c/b> model (pretraining only), the post-trained model with thinking switched off (an \u003cb>instruct\u003c/b> model) and the same model with thinking switched on (a \u003cb>reasoning\u003c/b> model). Qwen3-0.6B is small enough to run on a laptop CPU.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 or newer, about 6 GB of free RAM (both models are loaded in 32-bit floats on a CPU) and 4 GB of disk\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>pip install \"transformers&gt;=4.51\" torch\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>Models (downloaded automatically on first use): \u003ccode>Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B-Base\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>side_by_side.py\u003c/code>:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nPROMPTS = {\n    \"instruction\": \"Explain the moon landing to a 6 year old in three sentences.\",\n    \"few_shot\": \"Translate English to French:\\nsea otter =&gt; loutre de mer\\npeppermint =&gt; menthe poivrée\\ncheese =&gt;\",\n    \"maths\": \"Pens cost $2 for a pack of 3. How much do 18 pens cost? Put the final answer on the last line.\",\n    \"recent_fact\": \"Who won the 2026 FIFA World Cup final?\",\n}\n\ndef load(name):\n    tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\n    model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name)\n    return tok, model\n\ndef run(tok, model, text, max_new_tokens, **sampling):\n    inputs = tok(text, return_tensors=\"pt\")\n    start = time.perf_counter()\n    out = model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens=max_new_tokens, do_sample=True, **sampling)\n    new_tokens = out[0][inputs.input_ids.shape[1]:]\n    return tok.decode(new_tokens, skip_special_tokens=True), len(new_tokens), time.perf_counter() - start\n\ndef as_chat(tok, prompt, thinking):\n    return tok.apply_chat_template([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}], tokenize=False,\n                                   add_generation_prompt=True, enable_thinking=thinking)\n\nbase_tok, base = load(\"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B-Base\")\nchat_tok, chat = load(\"Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\")\n\nfor name, prompt in PROMPTS.items():\n    runs = {\n        \"base\": run(base_tok, base, prompt, 150, temperature=0.7, top_p=0.9),\n        \"instruct\": run(chat_tok, chat, as_chat(chat_tok, prompt, False), 300,\n                        temperature=0.7, top_p=0.8, top_k=20),\n        \"reasoning\": run(chat_tok, chat, as_chat(chat_tok, prompt, True), 2000,\n                         temperature=0.6, top_p=0.95, top_k=20),\n    }\n    for mode, (text, n_tokens, seconds) in runs.items():\n        print(f\"\\n===== {name} | {mode} | {n_tokens} tokens | {seconds:.1f}s =====\\n{text}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Run it: \u003ccode>python side_by_side.py | tee results.txt\u003c/code>. The reasoning runs take the longest; on a CPU expect a few minutes in total.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Print the raw chat-formatted prompt once to see what the model really receives: \u003ccode>print(as_chat(chat_tok, \"Hi\", True))\u003c/code>. Note the special tokens such as \u003ccode>&lt;|im_start|&gt;user\u003c/code> and the assistant turn left open for the model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Fill in a table: for each prompt and mode, did it follow the instruction, was it correct, how many tokens and seconds did it take?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run the script a second time. Sampling is random, so note which behaviours are stable and which change between runs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional: repeat the instruction and maths prompts on a large instruct model and a large reasoning model through any API or OpenRouter, and compare with the 0.6B results.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Base model:\u003c/b> treats every prompt as a document to continue. The instruction prompt may produce more questions, a list or a rambling story, because following instructions was never trained. The few-shot translation usually works: in-context learning comes from pretraining alone.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Instruct mode:\u003c/b> answers the question in a helpful format. This is what instruction tuning and RLHF added. On the maths prompt a model this small may still get it wrong (the answer is $12).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reasoning mode:\u003c/b> writes a \u003ccode>&lt;think&gt;\u003c/code> block first, often several times more tokens and seconds, and is more likely to reach $12 on the maths prompt. This is what reasoning training added, and it is also why reasoning costs more.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recent fact:\u003c/b> the final was played after this model was trained. Watch whether each mode invents a winner or says it cannot know; neither post-training nor reasoning adds knowledge the model never saw.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can the base model handle the few-shot translation prompt but not the plain instruction?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Continuing a pattern set up in a document is exactly what pretraining teaches, so few-shot prompts work. Treating a line of text as a request to answer is a behaviour learnt in post-training, which the base model never had.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The reasoning run used several times more tokens than the instruct run for the maths question. What does that mean for a production system?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Thinking tokens are billed as output tokens and add latency, so reasoning should be reserved for steps where the accuracy gain is worth the extra cost and wait, and switched off or turned down for simple tasks.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>"}]);
